 To find the maximum value of the function \(f(n)\) defined by:
\[f(n) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
n + 10 & \text{if $n < 10$}, \\
f(n - 5) & \text{if $n \ge 10$},
\end{array}
\right.\]
we need to understand how the function behaves.

For \(n < 10\), \(f(n) = n + 10\). The maximum value in this range is when \(n = 9\), so \(f(9) = 9 + 10 = 19\).

For \(n \ge 10\), \(f(n) = f(n - 5)\). This means that for any \(n \ge 10\), the function will eventually reduce to a value less than 10 to which we already have a definition. Since \(f(n - 5)\) is itself a call to the function with a smaller argument, it will continue to reduce until it reaches a value less than 10. Therefore, the maximum value will be the maximum value defined for \(n < 10\), which is 19.

Thus, the maximum value of \(f(n)\) is \(\boxed{19}\).

To confirm this, we can write a Python function to compute \(f(n)\) for any positive integer \(n\) and verify the result.
```python
def f(n):
    if n < 10:
        return n + 10
    else:
        return f(n - 5)

# Compute f(n) for a range of values to confirm the maximum value
max_value = max(f(n) for n in range(1, 21))  # Using a range from 1 to 20 for verification
print(max_value)
```

